1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:02,440
This is Meera.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:05,120
Today was the first day of her chai stall.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,733
She served chai all day, and the galla —

4
00:00:07,787 --> 00:00:10,240
her cash box — is full of notes.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:12,480
But once she pays for the milk, the sugar,

6
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,800
and the stall's rent — did Meera actually

7
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,160
make a profit today?

8
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:17,800
Or a loss?

9
00:00:19,401 --> 00:00:20,481
She doesn't know.

10
00:00:20,761 --> 00:00:21,721
And honestly?

11
00:00:22,641 --> 00:00:24,881
Most people who start a business don't either.

12
00:00:25,389 --> 00:00:27,469
By the end of this course, you'll be able

13
00:00:27,549 --> 00:00:30,189
to answer that question — for Meera, and

14
00:00:30,349 --> 00:00:31,629
for your own business.

15
00:00:37,576 --> 00:00:39,576
Accounting sounds scary.

16
00:00:39,816 --> 00:00:41,816
But it started with something you already

17
00:00:41,896 --> 00:00:43,576
know how to do — counting.

18
00:00:44,656 --> 00:00:47,296
Five thousand years ago, in Mesopotamia,

19
00:00:47,376 --> 00:00:49,696
farmers pressed little marks into clay to

20
00:00:49,776 --> 00:00:51,616
count their sheep and grain.

21
00:00:52,376 --> 00:00:54,696
About five hundred years ago, in Venice,

22
00:00:54,776 --> 00:00:57,576
a monk named Luca Pacioli wrote down a clever

23
00:00:57,656 --> 00:00:59,656
system for keeping track of money.

24
00:01:00,336 --> 00:01:02,416
And Indian traders have kept the same kind

25
00:01:02,496 --> 00:01:05,536
of books — the bahi-khata — for centuries.

26
00:01:06,296 --> 00:01:08,616
Today, the books live inside apps.

27
00:01:09,056 --> 00:01:10,936
But the idea hasn't changed.

28
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:13,667
Meet Khata.

29
00:01:13,947 --> 00:01:16,267
Khata is a ledger book, and it'll be your

30
00:01:16,427 --> 00:01:17,747
guide through this course.

31
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:19,487
Notice something?

32
00:01:20,327 --> 00:01:22,487
An open book has two sides.

33
00:01:22,527 --> 00:01:25,127
A left page, and a right page.

34
00:01:26,127 --> 00:01:26,727
Remember that.

35
00:01:27,467 --> 00:01:29,547
It's going to matter more than you think.

36
00:01:30,611 --> 00:01:33,811
Every business owner — a chai stall, a startup,

37
00:01:33,851 --> 00:01:36,451
a giant company — really wants the answers

38
00:01:36,531 --> 00:01:38,211
to just two questions.

39
00:01:38,891 --> 00:01:40,491
One: am I making money?

40
00:01:41,671 --> 00:01:44,391
Two: what do I own, and what do I owe?

41
00:01:45,871 --> 00:01:48,831
Accounting answers both, with two reports.

42
00:01:49,611 --> 00:01:51,371
The first is called the Profit and Loss

43
00:01:51,451 --> 00:01:52,331
statement.

44
00:01:52,811 --> 00:01:54,251
Think of it as a movie.

45
00:01:54,331 --> 00:01:56,891
It plays the story of a period — a day,

46
00:01:56,944 --> 00:01:59,131
a month, a year — and tells you whether

47
00:01:59,251 --> 00:02:01,211
you earned more than you spent.

48
00:02:02,291 --> 00:02:04,211
The second is the Balance Sheet.

49
00:02:04,291 --> 00:02:05,331
That's a photo.

50
00:02:05,911 --> 00:02:07,671
One moment, frozen.

51
00:02:08,511 --> 00:02:10,911
Everything you own, and everything you owe,

52
00:02:11,031 --> 00:02:11,871
right now.

53
00:02:12,951 --> 00:02:14,711
A movie, and a photo.

54
00:02:14,871 --> 00:02:16,951
Profit and Loss, and Balance Sheet.

55
00:02:17,591 --> 00:02:19,631
We'll build both for Meera, by the end of

56
00:02:19,711 --> 00:02:20,351
April.

57
00:02:21,417 --> 00:02:24,377
But first, the most important idea in this

58
00:02:24,537 --> 00:02:25,817
whole lesson.

59
00:02:25,897 --> 00:02:29,017
Meera and Meera's Chai are not the same.

60
00:02:29,967 --> 00:02:32,687
To an accountant, the stall is its own little

61
00:02:32,767 --> 00:02:33,647
person.

62
00:02:34,327 --> 00:02:36,727
It has its own money, its own things, its

63
00:02:36,807 --> 00:02:37,927
own debts.

64
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:40,627
So when Meera takes fifty thousand rupees

65
00:02:40,707 --> 00:02:43,187
out of her savings and puts it in the galla,

66
00:02:43,267 --> 00:02:45,587
the money now belongs to the stall.

67
00:02:46,227 --> 00:02:48,267
And the stall remembers that it came from

68
00:02:48,347 --> 00:02:49,227
Meera.

69
00:02:50,247 --> 00:02:52,527
Here's where most new founders slip.

70
00:02:53,107 --> 00:02:56,227
It's my stall, so its money is my money.

71
00:02:56,836 --> 00:02:57,356
Nope.

72
00:02:57,556 --> 00:02:59,636
If Meera pays her home electricity bill

73
00:02:59,716 --> 00:03:02,623
from the galla, that's not a stall expense.

74
00:03:03,349 --> 00:03:04,896
And if she buys sugar for the stall with

75
00:03:04,976 --> 00:03:07,296
her own phone, the stall still needs to

76
00:03:07,376 --> 00:03:08,376
know about it.

77
00:03:09,216 --> 00:03:10,069
Two people.

78
00:03:10,423 --> 00:03:11,636
Two sets of money.

79
00:03:12,576 --> 00:03:14,696
This is called the business entity idea,

80
00:03:14,736 --> 00:03:16,736
and every rule you'll learn sits on top

81
00:03:16,816 --> 00:03:17,296
of it.

82
00:03:18,344 --> 00:03:20,584
So what goes into the stall's books?

83
00:03:21,424 --> 00:03:24,064
Only transactions — events that change what

84
00:03:24,104 --> 00:03:26,704
the business has or owes, and that you can

85
00:03:26,784 --> 00:03:27,984
measure in rupees.

86
00:03:29,124 --> 00:03:30,124
Let's play a game.

87
00:03:30,324 --> 00:03:31,604
Is it a transaction?

88
00:03:32,073 --> 00:03:34,953
Meera sells a cup of chai for twenty rupees.

89
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:37,360
Yes!

90
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:39,800
Money came in, chai went out.

91
00:03:40,594 --> 00:03:42,594
Meera smiles at a customer.

92
00:03:44,153 --> 00:03:44,993
Lovely.

93
00:03:45,193 --> 00:03:46,873
But no rupees moved.

94
00:03:47,033 --> 00:03:48,393
Not a transaction.

95
00:03:48,959 --> 00:03:49,799
Your turn.

96
00:03:50,079 --> 00:03:52,159
Meera buys a bag of sugar.

97
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:56,000
Yes.

98
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,600
Money went out, sugar came in.

99
00:03:59,159 --> 00:04:00,119
And one more.

100
00:04:00,359 --> 00:04:02,679
A milk supplier calls to say hello.

101
00:04:05,833 --> 00:04:07,153
Not a transaction.

102
00:04:07,353 --> 00:04:08,833
Nothing changed hands.

103
00:04:09,113 --> 00:04:09,993
Yet.

104
00:04:11,161 --> 00:04:13,281
That's really all accounting is.

105
00:04:13,881 --> 00:04:16,441
You record every transaction, you sort them

106
00:04:16,521 --> 00:04:18,441
into the right buckets, and you sum them

107
00:04:18,521 --> 00:04:20,281
up into the movie and the photo.

108
00:04:21,461 --> 00:04:22,021
Record.

109
00:04:22,341 --> 00:04:22,741
Sort.

110
00:04:23,061 --> 00:04:23,981
Summarise.

111
00:04:25,061 --> 00:04:27,141
Over the next lessons, we'll follow Meera

112
00:04:27,221 --> 00:04:30,101
through her first month — one day at a time.

113
00:04:30,967 --> 00:04:33,527
So — accounting answers two questions.

114
00:04:34,327 --> 00:04:36,567
The movie tells you if you made money.

115
00:04:37,047 --> 00:04:39,767
The photo shows what you own and owe.

116
00:04:40,247 --> 00:04:42,487
And the business is its own person.

117
00:04:43,593 --> 00:04:44,233
Your turn.

118
00:04:44,393 --> 00:04:45,393
Three quick ones.

119
00:04:45,993 --> 00:04:46,513
One.

120
00:04:46,793 --> 00:04:48,713
Meera pays her personal phone bill from

121
00:04:48,833 --> 00:04:50,233
her own bank account.

122
00:04:50,313 --> 00:04:52,233
Does it go in the stall's books?

123
00:04:54,193 --> 00:04:54,753
Two.

124
00:04:54,793 --> 00:04:57,153
A customer asks the price of chai, then

125
00:04:57,233 --> 00:04:58,113
walks away.

126
00:04:58,473 --> 00:04:59,953
Transaction, or not?

127
00:05:01,913 --> 00:05:02,473
Three.

128
00:05:02,553 --> 00:05:04,473
Which report is the photo?

129
00:05:06,513 --> 00:05:08,433
Answers at the start of the next lesson.

130
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:12,600
Next time, Meera opens the galla with fifty

131
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:14,840
thousand rupees of her own savings.

132
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:16,440
So whose money is it now?
